course: dictionary
lesson: 4033
title: Clues about a gazelle
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A gazelle is a kind of animal, so it is a noun. You can work out its meaning from the words around it. «Gazelles are slim, fast antelopes of Africa and Asia that escape cheetahs and lions by running and dodging.» Slim, fast and antelope tell you what a gazelle is, and the cheetahs and lions tell you it is hunted.
explain: Ask yourself what word would fit. In «Grass is the savanna's bread: zebras, wildebeest and gazelles graze it», the clue is that gazelles are grazers, so they eat grass. Swap in your meaning to check: a gazelle is a small, slim, fast antelope, and that still makes sense in every sentence.
explain: Sometimes the word is used in a new way. People can call a graceful, fast runner a gazelle. Then the sentence shows what it means: a child who runs like a gazelle is quick and smooth, not an animal at all. Look at the whole sentence before you decide.
fact: A gazelle is a small, slim, fast antelope with curved horns.
fact: Gazelles live in Africa and Asia, often on grassland such as the African savanna.
fact: Gazelles are herbivores, which means they eat plants, and cheetahs and lions hunt them.
fact: «Pronking» or «stotting» means bouncing high on stiff legs, which may warn a predator «I am fit, don't chase me».
fact: A herd of gazelles is a group that moves together, and people can also call a graceful, fast runner a gazelle.
exercise: «Gazelles are slim, fast antelopes of Africa and Asia.» What is a gazelle? | *A small, slim, fast antelope | A type of tall grass | A river in Africa | A kind of big cat
exercise: «Grass is the savanna's bread: zebras, wildebeest and gazelles graze it.» What do gazelles do to the grass? | Count it | Burn it | *Graze on it | Dig it up
exercise: «Gazelles... escape cheetahs and lions by running and dodging.» Which word means to get away from danger? | Hunt | *Escape | Migrate | Graze
test: «...they sometimes bounce on stiff legs, called pronking or stotting.» What does pronking mean? | *Bouncing high on stiff legs | Sleeping in the shade | Swimming fast across a river | Eating grass quickly
test: «Each year more than a million wildebeest, with zebras and gazelles, follow the rain and fresh grass.» Why do they move? | *To follow the rain and fresh grass | To escape from school | To find a sunny beach | To find snow
exam: A boy wins the race and his coach says, «You ran like a gazelle!» What does the coach mean? | He is hunting for lions | He has curved horns on his head | *He ran with grace and speed | He ate a big plate of grass
